cover: /posters/kafka-on-the-shore.jpg
date: 2024-11-02
excerpt: >-
  Not just my favorite book ever, potentially my favorite piece of fiction
  ever.
---

Kafka on the Shore is a fascinating book. Written by the brilliant Haruki Murakami, it follows the stories of two characters, Kafka Tamura and Mr. Nakata. We start with Kafka, a 15 year old hoping to escape his tyrannical father. As he runs further and further from home, he learns more about himself, looking inwards through his conversations with The Boy Called Crow. The other side of this story follows Mr. Nakata, an elderly man who, following a traumatic air raid during World War 2 as a child, has lost the ability to read or write but can now talk to cats and make fish rain from the sky.

This sort of magical realism is what makes Murakami's work so unique. Somehow it never feels out of place. Mr. Nakata genuinely was talking with the cats, just as Kafka with The Boy Called Crow.

> Sometimes fate is like a small sandstorm that keeps changing directions. You change direction but the sandstorm chases you. You turn again, but the storm adjusts. Over and over you play this out, like some ominous dance with death just before dawn. Why? Because this storm isn't something that blew in from far away, something that has nothing to do with you. This storm is you.

At its essence, this is what Kafka on the Shore is. As the two characters' paths intertwine, they are constantly faced with challenges internally and externally. Mr. Nakata and Kafka seem to fulfill each other's destinies, while Miss Saeki's unresolved past is mirrored in Kafka's struggles. This sort of connecting of characters continues throughout the story, whether it be Mr. Nakata and Johnnie Walker's meeting, Mr. Nakata eventually meeting Miss Saeki, the spiritual dream-like state that Kafka enters when talking with the ghost of Miss Saeki and exploring her relationship with her own Tamura, or when Kafka talks with The Boy Called Crow. The split-personality motif runs throughout the narrative, suggesting that parts of ourselves often exist in conversation or conflict with one another.

As the story unfolds, more and more of the mystery is solved, but even more questions are asked, leaving us wondering where this will all end up. And it, kind of, doesn't. I think thats what makes this book so special. The characters are incredibly realistic and introspective. Each digging into a part of the human psyche. Whether it be long lost love (Miss Saeki), random interests (Mr. Nakata), fear of something (Kafka), the want for more (All), the love of music (Oshima). Each character is instrumental in giving the reader questions about themselves.

By the end of the book, we seemingly reach some conclusions. Kafka's decision to return home marks the end of his journey but raises questions about his emotional and psychological growth. Has he escaped his father's prophecy, or has he come to terms with it? Mr. Nakata's death is both a closure and a continuation. His role as a medium between worlds, bridging the supernatural and the mundane, ends, but it also allows others (like Kafka) to complete their journeys. The unresolved questions (Kafka's father's murder, the exact nature of Miss Saeki's relationship with Kafka, and the metaphysical plane Kafka enters) mirror the complexity of human consciousness and fate. The story leaves us with all of these questions, yet it never feels like an answer is required.

In the end, Kafka on the Shore is not just my favorite book ever, its potentially my favorite piece of fiction ever. The way Murakami is able to intertwine two brilliant characters together and have both stories have profound endings simultaneously is incredible writing. Each of the supporting characters are brilliant in their own right. At its core, Kafka on the Shore is an introspective journey. What makes you, you? It can be destiny. It can be fate. Maybe there is free will. It's not about finding definitive answers, but about accepting life's uncertainties, and maybe listening to some Prince on the way.
